must not swap an ordinary preview from a compact file card to an image row.

Image receipt uses an opaque theme surface and the image presentation of
`ReceiveProgress.tsx`. Waiting is static; a known zero remains accessible without
a visible percentage. Positive incomplete chunk counts drive a small radial
indicator, with phase-only live announcements. Complete chunk counts, local reads
and image loading retain a preparing state; file readiness alone does not reveal
the image. A current-source load reveals it with a brief opacity transition,
reduced with the user's motion preference. Cached geometry skips preparation on
remount. Load/error callbacks require current source and session ownership, and
preview failure belongs to its Blob URL so a replacement can recover. No receive
speed, stall cause, cancel action or thumbnail is inferred from chunk counts.
Ordinary file progress and attachment consent retain their existing presentation.

The ordinary-image lightbox owns pointer-anchored wheel zoom (1–8 times the
window-fit size), double-click zoom, bounded pointer dragging, fit reset and
keyboard zoom (+/−/0). Resize clamps the current position; reopening resets it.
Controls remain outside the clipped image stage. Escape restores trigger focus.
On macOS, unzoomed images retain native text selection; Option/Alt-drag bypasses
panning when zoomed. Actual recognition and selection require WKWebView evidence.
Ordinary-image context actions use the shared accessible menu, suppress WebKit
browser defaults and reuse the attachment save/reveal owners. `ui/lib/imageClipboard.ts`
exports the entire decoded image as PNG, starts the write within user activation,
and guards delayed encoding with current-session authority. Image copying honors
confirmation and best-effort unchanged-content clearing. File sharing is exposed
only after a platform capability check; native subject extraction is not exposed.
Escape dismisses the action menu before dismissing the preview.
Attachment consent remains owned by `FileBubble` and the existing file policy.

`mediaFile.tsx` owns durable-first reads and explicitly opts ordinary previews
into `mediaObjectUrlCache.ts`. Concurrent consumers share one admitted read and
receiving it. Inline images keep their existing in-app preview behavior.

## Find received files

Use **Received files** in the sidebar to browse locally known file entries. If a transfer is waiting, check consent and connectivity before looking for a completed file on disk.

Completed downloads provide **Reveal**. If writing the download fails,
check that the destination is writable, then use the file's retry action.

## Copy text from an image

In the macOS desktop app, click an ordinary image in the conversation to enlarge
it. Move the pointer over the words, pause briefly, then drag to select the text.
Press **Command-C**, or right-click the selection and choose **Copy text**. Recognition
uses macOS Live Text and depends on the system's support for the image and language.
When zoomed in, hold **Option** while dragging to select text rather than pan.

## Image actions

Right-click an enlarged ordinary image to open the app's image menu:

- **Copy image** copies the whole decoded image as PNG, independent of zoom or pan.
  This action appears when the platform provides image clipboard support. If the
// Lightweight, dependency-free platform detection for chrome and font adjustments.
//
// The custom title bar (tauri.conf `titleBarStyle: "Overlay"`) is macOS-only: the native
// traffic-lights float over the top-left of our content, so the sidebar header needs a
// top/left inset there — and ONLY there. On Windows/Linux the native title bar is kept,
// so no inset (it would just be awkward empty space). In a plain browser (the Playwright
// e2e harness, dev in a tab) there are no traffic-lights either, so no inset.
//
// We mark the document root with `data-os="macos"` only when BOTH: we're inside the Tauri
// webview (`__TAURI_INTERNALS__`) AND the platform looks like a Mac. CSS keys off it.

function isTauri(): boolean {
  return (
    typeof window !== "undefined" &&
    "__TAURI_INTERNALS__" in (window as unknown as Record<string, unknown>)
  );
}

function looksLikeMac(): boolean {
  if (typeof navigator === "undefined") return false;
  // The macOS WebView (WKWebView) user-agent always contains "Macintosh" / "Mac OS X".
  // (We avoid the deprecated `navigator.platform`.)
  return /Mac OS X|Macintosh/i.test(navigator.userAgent || "");
}

/** True when the macOS overlay title bar is in effect (Tauri + Mac). */
export function isMacOverlay(): boolean {
  return isTauri() && looksLikeMac();
}

/**
